<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ballari</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bellary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ballari, formerly Bellary, is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, known for the fort on the great granite rock that overlooks it. The surrounding region, rich in iron ore, has a long history: the earliest Pallava charters of the fourth century record grants of land there, and it later lay in the central lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, whose capital Hampi is nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ballari.jpg" alt="Ballari" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ravibhalli · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BELLARY_FORT_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ballari stands in the middle of a wide, level plain of black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> soil, and granite rocks and hills are a prominent feature of the landscape. The city is spread mainly around two hills of granite, the Ballari Hill and the Kumbara Gudda. Ballari Hill has a circumference of nearly 2 miles and a height of 480 feet. To the east and south lies an irregular heap of boulders, to the west an unbroken monolith, and the north is walled by bare, rugged ridges. Kumbara Gudda looks like the profile of a human face when seen from the south-east, and is also called Face Hill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ballari was part of Rayalaseema, the Ceded Districts, which belonged to the Madras Presidency until 1 November 1956. The city was known as Bellary until it was renamed Ballari on 1 November 2014.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a> was historically one of the main crops of the surrounding country, and the city developed ginning, spinning and weaving. The earliest steam cotton-spinning mill was established in 1894, and by 1901 it had 17,800 spindles and employed 520 hands. The garment industry dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> tailors of the Darji community migrated from the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> region to stitch uniforms for the soldiers of the British Indian Army stationed at Ballari. After the war the community turned to school uniforms, and the uniforms made there gradually became popular throughout the country.</p><p>Ballari is now well known for its denim garments, made in about 260 units where nearly 3,000 families work. The city municipal council was upgraded to a city corporation in 2004, and the surrounding district is also known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industry, which has given it the name of the steel city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ballari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Pallavas first appear in the late third and fourth centuries CE in copper-plate charters written in Prakrit, such as the Mayidavolu and Hirahadagalli grants of Shivaskandavarman, issued from Kanchipuram and recording gifts of land in the Andhra country and the <b>Bellary</b> region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mining around Sandur in <b>Ballari</b> district remains important.</blockquote><a href="/state/sandur-state">Sandur State</a></li></ul></section>
